@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@command{starter-explain} is a long-running daemon that attaches to
@command{starter-core}'s remote debug server (GDBus over TCP).  It tracks
I/O state grouped into @dfn{contexts} defined in an XML configuration
(@file{.stex.xml}), and on relevant changes it:

@enumerate
@item
Builds a structured prompt for an external large language model (LLM).
@item
Converts the model reply to speech via an external text-to-speech (TTS)
service.
@item
Plays the resulting audio on the host (for example with @command{paplay}).
@end enumerate

The daemon does not embed an LLM or a RAG index.  Those are separate
processes (Ollama, a thin RAG HTTP wrapper, Wyoming Piper, and so on).
@command{starter-explain} only needs HTTP (or Wyoming TCP) endpoints and a
valid stex file.

I/O human-readable text in prompts comes from the @strong{description}
field published by the debug interface, not from duplicated labels in the
stex file.  Context @code{name} / @code{description} attributes describe
the group, not each individual I/O.

@node Building and Running
@chapter Building and Running

@section Prerequisites

@itemize
@item GLib / GObject / GIO (@code{libglib2.0-dev})
@item libxml2 (@code{libxml2-dev})
@item json-glib (@code{libjson-glib-dev})
@item libsoup 3.x (or 2.4 @math{\ge} 2.58)
@item Autotools (@code{autoconf}, @code{automake}, @code{libtool})
@item Optional: @code{texinfo} to build this manual
@end itemize

@section Build

@example
$ ./autogen.sh
$ ./configure
$ make
$ sudo make install
@end example

@section Command line

@example
$ starter-explain --config=examples/demo.stex.xml
$ starter-explain --config=FILE --core=HOST:PORT --dry-run
@end example

@table @code
@item --config=FILE
Path to the stex XML configuration (required).
@item --core=HOST:PORT
Override the @code{<core>} host and port from the file.
@item --dry-run
Build prompts and call the LLM/TTS path without playing audio
(or skip side effects as implemented).
@end table

Enable verbose logs with:

@example
$ G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all starter-explain --config=FILE
@end example

@node Configuration Reference
@chapter Configuration Reference

The root element is @code{<stex version="1">}.  Comments in
@file{examples/demo.stex.xml} mirror this chapter.

@section Core

@example
<core host="127.0.0.1" port="17373"/>
@end example

Host and TCP port of starter-core's remote debug server.

@section LLM

@example
<llm url="http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/generate"
     model="qwen2.5:3b"
     timeout-ms="30000"
     max-queue="4"
     num-predict="32"
     keep-alive="300">
  <system>...</system>
  <prompt>
    <user-template>...</user-template>
  </prompt>
</llm>
@end example

@table @code
@item url
HTTP endpoint.  The daemon POSTs an Ollama-compatible JSON body
(@code{model}, @code{system}, @code{prompt}, @code{stream:false},
@code{options}, optional @code{keep_alive}).
@item model
Model name forwarded to the endpoint.
@item num-predict
Maximum tokens to generate (voice replies should stay short).
@item keep-alive
Seconds to keep the model loaded (@code{-1} = server default / forever).
@item system
System message text.
@item user-template
User prompt template.  Placeholders include @code{@{@{type@}@}},
@code{@{@{event@}@}}, @code{@{@{rule@}@}}, @code{@{@{context@}@}},
@code{@{@{state@}@}}, plus legacy aliases documented in the source.
@end table

Optional @code{<header name="..." value="..."/>} children may be added
under @code{<llm>} for HTTP authentication.

@section TTS and audio

Wyoming (recommended for Piper):

@example
<tts method="wyoming"
     url="tcp://127.0.0.1:5005"
     timeout-ms="30000"
     max-queue="4"
     response="audio/wav"/>
<audio player="paplay @{@{file@}@}"/>
@end example

@code{method="wyoming"} (or a @code{tcp://} / @code{wyoming://} URL) uses
the Wyoming protocol.  PCM is wrapped as a temporary WAV file; then the
@code{player} command is run with @samp{@{@{file@}@}} replaced.

HTTP TTS uses @code{method="POST"} (or @code{GET}) with
@code{body-template} / @code{content-type} as needed.

@section Ignore, contexts, deadband, policy

@table @asis
@item @code{<ignore>}
Patterns or names that never trigger an explanation.
@item @code{<contexts>} / @code{<context>}
Named groups of @code{<io name="..."/>} or @code{pattern="..."}.
An I/O may belong to several contexts.
@item @code{<deadband>}
Per-I/O or pattern @code{min-delta} overrides.
@item @code{<policy>}
@code{debounce-ms}, @code{coalesce}, @code{history-length}, default
@code{min-delta}.
@end table

@node Prompt Contract
@chapter Prompt Contract

The default user template emits:

@example
TYPE: event|action
EVENT: ...
RULE: ... | none
CONTEXT: ...
STATE:
...
@end example

@table @code
@item TYPE
@code{action} when an engine RULE/TRIGGER describe is available for the
change; otherwise @code{event} (including output changes without a cached
rule, which still go to the LLM with @code{RULE: none}).
@item EVENT
Changed I/O id, direction, old/new values, unit, and debug description.
@item RULE
Text from @code{RuleTriggered} / @code{TriggerTriggered} on the destination,
or @code{none}.
@item CONTEXT
Merged context names and descriptions for the changed I/O.
@item STATE
Compact snapshot: the changed I/O (with short history), and for actions
any I/O identifiers referenced by the rule text.
@end table

Tune the @code{<system>} message so the model follows event vs action
style (third person vs ``I @dots{} because @dots{}'').

@node External Services
@chapter External Services

@section Ollama

Point @code{<llm url>} at @code{http://HOST:11434/api/generate}, or at a
wrapper that accepts the same JSON and returns
@code{@{ "response": "..." @}}.

@section Optional RAG

A separate HTTP service may retrieve short curated documents before
calling Ollama.  That service is @strong{not} part of the
@command{starter-explain} binary.  Keep manuals and large dumps out of the
voice corpus if they pollute short spoken replies.

@section Wyoming Piper

Run a Wyoming TTS server (for example @code{rhasspy/wyoming-piper}) and
set @code{<tts method="wyoming" url="tcp://HOST:PORT"/>}.

@node Demo Walkthrough
@chapter Demo Walkthrough

@file{examples/demo.stex.xml} is a localhost configuration with English
comments on every block.  To try it:

@enumerate
@item
Start starter-core with remote debug on @code{127.0.0.1:17373}
(or pass @code{--core=} to match your setup).
@item
Start an LLM endpoint (Ollama or compatible) and optionally Wyoming TTS.
@item
Adjust @code{model}, URLs, and context I/O names to match your rules and
adapters.
@item
Run:

@example
$ starter-explain --config=examples/demo.stex.xml --dry-run
@end example

@item
Change an I/O that belongs to a context and watch the log for the built
prompt and LLM reply.
@end enumerate

Map your real devices by listing their debug I/O names under the right
@code{<context>}, set ignore patterns for noisy meters, and tune
deadbands so humidity or temperature chatter does not spam the voice path.

